ks like weakness or a float. Better to either:\n- Check (0) — passive, low risk\n- Or bet meaningfully: 600 (half-pot) or so\n\nBut you only have 19,500 - 600 already bet = 18,900 left? Wait: you had 19,500 *at start of hand*. You already put in 600 (200 initial, 400 call), so remaining chips: 19,500 - 600 = 18,900.\n\nSo you can bet up to 18,900.\n\nBut optimal move: **semi-bluff with a solid bet** or **check-call**.\n\nGiven opponent showed strength pre-flop, they likely have a King or overpairs. If they have Kx, they’ll likely bet the flop. So **checking to them is better** — you can call or raise their bet.\n\nIf you bet now, they may just fold weaker hands or call with better ones. But you don’t want to chase out worse draws or induce bluffs.\n\nBest approach: **Check (0)** to control pot size and gather information.\n\nGoing all-in (-2) is too aggressive now — you’re not committed yet.\n\nRaising small (100) looks like a draw probe, but invites 3-bet or push.\n\nThus, safest and most strategic: **check**.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a strong flush draw with 9 outs and decent equity (~36% by river), but your hand is currently weak (no pair). Your opponent likely has a King or strong hand given their pre-flop re-raise. Checking allows you to see a turn card cheaply or respond to their aggression. It avoids bloating the pot when behind, while preserving your ability to continue when improving. A small raise (100) is too weak to be meaningful, and larger bets risk overcommitting without showdown value. Thus, checking is the most balanced and EV-positive play.\n\nAnswer: 0'}
