ps.].\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Evaluation:  \nYou hold the **8♠ and Q♠**, and the board shows **K♦, 2♠, 4♠, 9♥**. This means four spades are already out (you have Q♠ and 8♠, and the board has 2♠ and 4♠), so there are **two spades in your hand and two on the board** — giving you **four to a flush**. Only one more spade is needed to complete a **flush**, which would be a very strong hand. Additionally, while less likely, a straight is possible with a 5 or 10 on the river (e.g., 5-6-7-8-9 or 8-9-10-J-Q), but currently not close.\n\nYour opponent has shown strength by raising pre-flop and calling through flop and turn. They may have top pair (like Kx), middle pair, or even two pair. However, they haven’t bet aggressively on the turn (checked), suggesting hesitation or a medium-strength hand.\n\nYou are on a **flush draw** with one card to come. The odds of hitting a spade on the river are about **~19%** (9 outs ÷ 46 unseen cards). Pot odds matter here, but since both players checked the turn, the pot size isn't growing yet. Checking now allows you to **see the river for free**, which is ideal when drawing. Raising would risk building the pot without having made your hand yet, and could cause your opponent to fold weaker hands you might outdraw, or worse — get re-raised by a stronger hand.\n\nGoing all-in might protect a draw, but it’s high-risk here; your opponent could easily fold worse or call with a made hand that beats a completed flush (e.g., full house). Folding is too weak — you still have a live draw and position via action.\n\nBest move is to **check**, keeping the pot manageable and seeing if you hit your flush on the river.\n\nReason:  \nChecking preserves your chip stack, avoids committing further without improvement, and allows you to see the river card at no additional cost. If a spade hits, you can then evaluate betting or raising based on your opponent's action. If it misses, you can fold or bluff depending on context. This maximizes expected valu